<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/expert/formal-political-theory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Formal political theory is <strong>politics as mathematics</strong> — voters, parties and
    states as players in precisely specified games. It produced the field's most durable
    results (median voter, Arrow's limits, veto-player gridlock) and its most practical exports
    (auction and matching design, the economics lesson's Nobel-winning mechanism design).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="median voter, impossibility, spatial models, mechanism design" widget="equationStep" config={formalCfg} />

  <h2>The method's strengths and honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strength — assumptions are visible:</strong> a formal model states exactly what it assumes about preferences and information. Disagreement becomes precise (which assumption fails?) instead of rhetorical.</li>
    <li><strong>Limit — people aren\'t always rational:</strong> behavioural findings (the economics lesson's biases) break rational-choice predictions; turnout itself is a puzzle (rational voter should rarely vote — one vote almost never matters). The field integrates the anomaly rather than denying it.</li>
    <li><strong>The empirical turn:</strong> formal models now pair with experiments and field data — lab voting experiments, big-data tests of spatial predictions. Theory generates hypotheses; the statistics ladder's tools test them.</li>
    <li><strong>Why non-mathematicians should care:</strong> every constitution, electoral rule and treaty is a mechanism. Whether it produces the intended outcome is a formal question — these are the rules of the rules.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Downs, An Economic Theory of Democracy (1957); Arrow (1951); Tsebelis, Veto Players (2002); Roth on market design.</p>
</TopicPage>
